## Month 1 – Networking Fundamentals

In the first month of my cybersecurity journey, I learned many of the networking fundamentals required to understand how the internet works. This included topics like:
* DNS, Subnetting, OSI Model, and TCP/IP Model
* DHCP, Routing, NAT, PAT, and IP Addressing
* ARP, LAN, VLAN, and Switches

I also did some lab work with Kali Linux where I learned commands like `traceroute`, `ip a`, `ip route`, `ifconfig`, `ping`, and some `nmap` commands.

When I learned the fundamentals of networking, everything became way clearer to me and pretty easy to understand—like seeing my own IP and the subnet mask.

Wireshark is a great tool to capture network traffic which actually made me understand the network flow even better , with wireshark i observed the dns queries , tcp handshake , tls handshake , 
https traffic.

Nmap is also a good tool that is used to scan ip addresses to identify a device , OS , service , service versions etc.
In short nmap is a network discovery tool.
some common nmap commands are :
nmap -sS  192.168.1.1
nmap -sV  192.168.1.1
nmap -A   192.168.1.1
nmap -Pn  192.168.1.1
nmap -sU  192.168.1.1
nmap -sT  192.168.1.1
nmap -p 1-1000  192.168.1.1

There are many other nmap commands that are used for different purposes.














